<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Healing Plants of Panama – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose a simple thank-you letter with support. Interactive: Reply to a message written on paper, in a text app, or in an email.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a thank-you letter to someone who shared plant remedies (e.g., "Thank you for teaching us about guanabana (soursop)."). Can respond to a text message about plant uses in a familiar way (e.g., "Sara will remember to use sabila (aloe vera) for burns."). Can reply with simple feedback to a message about medicinal plants (e.g., "Thank you for the information on sabila (aloe vera).").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of various medicinal plants (aloe vera, soursop, lemongrass, mint leaves, ginger) on the board. Students are asked to write down one word they associate with each plant (e.g., "Aloe vera - burns"). Then, they write one sentence to complete: "Sábila is helpful for _______." This activates prior knowledge and sets a writing-focused mood.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple advice sentences on the board using the target grammar. For example: "You should use sábila (aloe vera) for burns." or "Hojas de menta (mint leaves) are used to relieve headaches." The teacher stresses compound nouns like "mint leaves" and models syllable division for words like "treatment" and "therapeutic" by clapping or marking syllables on the board, linking pronunciation to written form. The teacher then models how to write a short, simple thank-you phrase related to the plants.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I have a headache, should I use aloe vera or mint leaves?" (Expected answer: "Mint leaves.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is sábila used to treat?" (Expected answer: "Burns.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, authentic-looking text, like an email or a message board post from a local community group discussing traditional remedies. It will be a "short notice" or "informal message" about shared plant knowledge. The text will contain examples of infinitive of purpose, should/shouldn't, and present simple, along with target vocabulary (e.g., "Hi everyone! My grandma told me that guanábana is used to treat infections. You should drink the tea. Also, sábila is really useful for burns. You should apply it carefully. Thanks for sharing your tips!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Present and exchange simple health-related advice and remedies in discussions or role-play scenarios (adapted for written exchange).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the message and identify two plants mentioned and their uses. (Gist question: "What two plants are mentioned in the message?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False statements about the message (e.g., "Guanábana is used for burns. (False)").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students underline sentences showing advice ("should") and purpose ("used to").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Writing.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Infinitive of purpose: used to + infinitive verb; Should/shouldn't for giving advice; Present simple for facts. Vocabulary: plant, leaf, root, remedy, ginger, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves, treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare, useful, medicinal, natural, therapeutic, for, with, traditionally, safely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks focusing on the target grammar and vocabulary. Students complete the sentences individually. E.g., "Sábila is _______ to _______ burns." (used, soothe/treat); "You _______ use ginger tea for colds." (should); "Lemongrass _______ leaves are _______ for colds." (plant, good). Students practice writing out the full sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Guided Sentence Writing):</b> Students are given prompts related to different plants and are asked to write one sentence using "used to" and one sentence using "should/shouldn't."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 1: Guanábana (soursop) / infections. (e.g., "Guanábana is used to treat infections. You should drink the juice.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 2: Mint leaves / headaches. (e.g., "Mint leaves are used to relieve headaches. You should boil them for tea.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 3: Aloe vera / burns. (e.g., "Aloe vera is used to heal burns. You should apply the gel.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Writing & Peer Check - Advice Exchange):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes three pieces of advice about medicinal plants they learned, using "You should/shouldn't" and "is used to" (e.g., "You should use sábila for burns." "Ginger is used to treat colds."). They then exchange their notes with their partner, checking for correct grammar, vocabulary, and clarity. They provide simple written feedback (e.g., "Good advice!" or "Check verb after 'used to'.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-17 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario:</b> "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Written product – composing a short thank-you letter and a short reply message.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part A: Thank-You Letter:</b> Students imagine they have received information about plant remedies from a local expert or a community elder. They will write a simple thank-you letter to this person. The letter should:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Express thanks (e.g., "Thank you for teaching us about...").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mention at least two specific plants and their uses (e.g., "Guanábana is used to treat infections.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Include one piece of advice using "should" (e.g., "We should remember to use sábila (aloe vera) for burns.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use appropriate vocabulary (plant, remedy, treat, useful, apply).</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher provides a simple template or sentence starters for support (e.g., "Dear [Name], Thank you for... I learned that [plant] is used to... We should also know that... Sincerely, [Your Name]").
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part B: Reply to a Message:</b> Students read a short message (provided by the teacher) from a friend asking for a reminder about a plant remedy (e.g., "Hey, I forgot what sábila is good for. Can you remind me?"). Students then write a short reply (as if in a text app or email) confirming the information and maybe adding a simple piece of advice (e.g., "Hi [Friend's Name]! Sabila is helpful for burns. You should apply it on your skin. Sara will remember to use sabila (aloe vera) for burns!").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Focus is on communicative completeness and correct application of target grammar and vocabulary in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Student Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written thank-you letters and reply messages with a new partner. Each student uses a simple checklist to assess their partner's writing. The teacher circulates, observing students and providing immediate feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (on checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Letter expresses thanks clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes at least two plants and their uses (e.g., "Guanábana is used to treat infections.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes advice using "should/shouldn't" (e.g., "You should use sábila (aloe vera) for burns.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (plant, remedy, treat, apply, useful).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reply message answers the question about the plant use.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall clarity and legibility.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One new thing I learned to write today is...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can write advice about plant remedies using...</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the most challenging part of writing the thank-you letter or reply message today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can knowing about healing plants help people in Panama?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully composed simple thank-you letters and replies, incorporating "used to" and "should." The guided writing activities in Stage 3 were crucial for building confidence. Some students still need more practice with subject-verb agreement in the present simple. The peer assessment worked well for immediate feedback, but I need to ensure students understand the criteria fully. I will prepare more sentence frames for the next writing task to further scaffold those who struggled.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This writing lesson has prepared students to express information about plant remedies. In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize information about healing plants, building on their ability to structure and communicate information in written form. They might explain a traditional remedy to someone who doesn't understand Spanish, utilizing the English vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>